  "query_preview": "..\nNot expecting to see any major weather events this coming week. \nHowever, as high pressure in the Gulf breaks down, several minor \ndisturbances will move through the area, leading to the \ndevelopment of showery precipitation. By Sunday, the lingering \nremnants of a front will support rain showers primarily in the \nnorthern panhandle. 24 hour rainfall amounts on Sunday are \nexpected to be around 0.1\u201d, with the highest rates occurring \novernight into Sunday morning. Most precipitation will be isolated\nto higher elevations.\n\nSomething of note, some models are indicating favorable lower \nlevel lapse rates along the international border, which may help \nthunderstorm development at higher elevations from Sunday through \nTuesday. However, limited shear and a lack of instability in the \nmid to upper levels should inhibit deep convection. Most if not \nall storms are expected to remain in Canada rather than crossing \nthe Coast Range into our AOR.\n\nThrough the weekend, a weak upper level trough is expected to \nmove over the eastern Gulf, followed by the formation of a jet \nstreak south of the region by Tuesday. This, combined with a low-\npressure center tracking east from the Bering Sea across the Gulf,\nmay provide sufficient energy for widespread rain showers and \nhigher QPF amounts as a front pushes northward on Tuesday. While \nguidance is beginning to agree on the track of the Gulf low, there\nis still large spread in rainfall totals, which will likely be \nmore influenced by local effects than anything else.",
